  "verbalization": "Ixoside is implicated in the interaction with Phosphodiesterase 6C through a series of molecular targets, all of which are classified as MIPs (Molecular Interaction Proteins). These MIPs are identified as targets of Ixoside, suggesting a direct pharmacological engagement at the molecular level. The evidence is consistent across three distinct pathways, each linking Ixoside to a different MIP that ultimately targets Phosphodiesterase 6C. This suggests a general mechanism of action where Ixoside interacts with a family of compounds that share a common target, Phosphodiesterase 6C, potentially through a shared binding site or regulatory pathway. The presence of multiple MIPs as targets of Ixoside indicates a broader pharmacological profile, possibly involving multiple molecular interactions that converge on the same downstream effect. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Ixoside exerts its biological activity by modulating Phosphodiesterase 6C, likely through a mechanism that involves the inhibition of enzyme activity or other regulatory processes.",
